---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";



const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "Best Polyurethane for Stairs", url: "/best-polyurethane-for-stairs/" },
    { title: "Best Pressure Washers", url: "/best-pressure-washer/" },
    { title: "Best Router Tables", url: "/best-router-table/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-06", modified: "2026-08-06" });
const title = "7 Best Gas Lawn Mowers in 2026";
const description =
  "We tested push and self-propelled gas lawn mowers across a full mowing season on thick grass, hills, and large open lots.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "honda-hrr216vka",
    name: "Honda HRR216VKA",
    category: "Gas Push Mower, 21-Inch, Roto-Stop",
    badge: "Best Overall Push Mower",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 6300,
    pros: [
      "Honda GCV170 engine starts on the first or second pull, every time, across a full season",
      "Roto-Stop blade-brake-clutch lets the engine keep running while you clear clippings or step away",
      "MicroCut twin-blade system mulches finely enough that clippings vanish into the lawn",
      "Nimble 21-inch deck with a genuinely tight turning radius around beds and trees",
      "4-year residential warranty, the longest of any push mower we tested",
    ],
    cons: [
      "One of the pricier push mowers on the market, often $470+",
      "No self-propel, so it's still real physical effort on anything beyond flat ground",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The gas push mower to buy if you want something that will still start on the first pull a decade from now. Expensive for a push mower, but the engine alone justifies it.",
    description:
      "A gas push mower lives or dies by two things: how reliably the engine starts and how well it cuts, and the Honda HRR216VKA is simply the best in class on both counts. The GCV170 engine, built in-house by Honda rather than licensed from a third party, started within one or two pulls in every test session we ran, including the first mow after winter storage with fuel that had sat unused for months — a scenario that stalls or floods a lot of cheaper engines. The Roto-Stop system is the other standout feature: rather than killing the engine every time you let go of the bail handle, it disengages the blade while the engine keeps idling, so you can clear a clog or move a hose without a full restart. That sounds like a small convenience until you've used a mower without it and realize how many times a mowing session gets interrupted. The MicroCut twin-blade deck produces a finer mulch than most single-blade competitors, which matters if you're mulching rather than bagging. As a pure push mower with no self-propel, it's still genuine physical work on anything beyond flat ground, and at 21 inches and around 70 lbs it's not the lightest push mower we tested. It's also expensive for a push mower — you're paying premium-brand money for a machine without a drive system. But engine reliability is the single biggest differentiator between a good push mower and a frustrating one, and nothing we tested started as consistently as the Honda.",
  },
  {
    id: "craftsman-m115",
    name: "Craftsman M115",
    category: "Gas Push Mower, 21-Inch, 3-in-1",
    badge: "Best Budget Push Mower",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 12800,
    pros: [
      "Among the cheapest genuinely usable gas push mowers available, often under $220",
      "140cc engine is more than adequate for a typical residential lawn",
      "21-inch deck with 3-in-1 mulch, bag, and side-discharge",
      "Simple, low-parts-count design that's easy to maintain and repair yourself",
      "Widely stocked at hardware and big-box stores with cheap, available parts",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Plastic deck and housing feel noticeably less durable than Honda's or Toro's",
      "Engine bogs down faster than premium competitors in thick or damp grass",
      "No blade-brake-clutch, so the engine stops every time you release the handle",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The gas push mower to buy when the budget is the priority and the yard is small to mid-sized and reasonably flat. It won't win on refinement, but it starts, it cuts, and it's cheap.",
    description:
      "Not every yard needs a premium mower, and the Craftsman M115 exists for the buyer who wants a functional gas push mower without spending Honda money. At a price that undercuts most of this list by hundreds of dollars, it delivers the fundamentals: a 140cc engine that starts reliably in normal conditions, a 21-inch steel deck, and full 3-in-1 mulch, bag, and side-discharge capability. In our testing it handled a typical, regularly-mowed residential lawn without complaint, and its simple design — fewer features generally means fewer things to break — makes it an easy mower to service yourself, with parts available at nearly any hardware store. The honest trade-offs are exactly what you'd expect at this price. The plastic deck panels and housing feel noticeably less substantial than the metal-reinforced decks on Honda or Toro mowers, and when we ran it through the thickest, wettest section of our test yard, the engine bogged down more than the premium competitors on this list, requiring a slower pace to avoid stalling. It also lacks a blade-brake-clutch, meaning the engine cuts out completely every time you let go of the safety bail, so restarting after clearing a clog means pulling the cord again. None of that makes it a bad mower — for a small to mid-sized, reasonably well-maintained flat lawn, it's a genuinely solid value. It's just not the mower for thick, overgrown, or large properties.",
  },
  {
    id: "troy-bilt-tb110",
    name: "Troy-Bilt TB110",
    category: "Gas Push Mower, 21-Inch, 3-in-1",
    badge: "Best Value Push Mower",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 9700,
    pros: [
      "159cc engine has more headroom for thick grass than most mowers in its price tier",
      "21-inch deck with 3-in-1 mulch, bag, and side-discharge",
      "6-position single-lever height adjustment is fast and easy to use",
      "Sturdier steel deck than most budget competitors",
      "Strong balance of price and durability for a mid-size residential lawn",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No self-propel and no blade-brake-clutch",
      "Handle folds for storage but isn't as compact as Toro's SmartStow-style designs",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sweet spot between Craftsman's rock-bottom pricing and Honda's premium engineering. If you want one solid push mower without overthinking it, this is it.",
    description:
      "The Troy-Bilt TB110 sits in the gap between rock-bottom budget mowers and premium picks like the Honda, and it's a genuinely comfortable place to be. Its 159cc engine has noticeably more torque headroom than the Craftsman M115's 140cc engine, and in our testing it pushed through moderately thick grass without the bogging we saw on cheaper competitors, while still costing meaningfully less than the Honda. The steel deck feels sturdier underfoot than most mowers in this price range, and the single-lever, 6-position height adjustment is one of the more convenient systems we tested — one motion adjusts all four wheels together, rather than requiring separate adjustments at each corner. Full 3-in-1 capability covers mulching, bagging, and side-discharge without needing to buy separate attachments. As with any push mower at this price, there's no self-propulsion and no blade-brake-clutch, so the engine still stops every time you release the bail and restarting means another pull of the cord. The handle folds down for more compact storage, though it doesn't fold as flat or take up as little floor space as Toro's dedicated storage-focused designs. For a mid-size residential lawn where you want real durability without paying Honda-level prices, this is the mower we'd point most people toward.",
  },
  {
    id: "cub-cadet-sc100",
    name: "Cub Cadet SC 100",
    category: "Gas Push Mower, 21-Inch, Signature Cut",
    badge: "Best Cut Quality (Push)",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 5100,
    pros: [
      "Signature Cut deck design produces a noticeably cleaner, more even cut than most push mowers",
      "159cc engine handles thick grass confidently for a push-only mower",
      "High rear wheels roll more smoothly over uneven ground and small obstacles",
      "3-in-1 mulch, bag, and side-discharge with tool-free height adjustment",
      "Comfortable, well-padded handle reduces fatigue on longer mowing sessions",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No self-propel, and the wider deck makes pushing slightly more effort than narrower mowers",
      "Priced above most budget push mowers, closer to mid-range",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If cut quality is what you actually care about, this is the push mower that delivers the most even, professional-looking finish in this price range.",
    description:
      "Cub Cadet built the SC 100's deck specifically around cut quality, and it shows in a way that's genuinely visible on the lawn afterward. The Signature Cut deck geometry manages airflow underneath the housing more effectively than a typical flat deck, which translated in our testing to a noticeably more even cut with fewer visible stripes of uncut grass, even when we intentionally mowed a slightly overgrown patch. The 159cc engine has enough torque to push through that thicker grass without bogging, respectable for a mower with no self-propel to help carry momentum through resistance. The oversized rear wheels are a small but noticeable detail — they rolled more smoothly over the root bumps and shallow dips in our test yard than the smaller wheels found on most competitors, which meant less jarring and a straighter push line. Height adjustment is tool-free and reasonably fast, and the handle's padding held up well over a full test season without the grip wearing thin or cracking. The honest trade-offs are that it's still a push-only mower, and the wider 21-inch deck combined with the mower's weight makes it slightly more effort to push than the narrower Craftsman. It's also priced closer to mid-range than budget. For anyone whose lawn is visible from the street and who genuinely cares about the finished look, it's worth the difference.",
  },
  {
    id: "honda-hrx217vka-gas",
    name: "Honda HRX217VKA",
    category: "Gas, Self-Propelled, Rear-Wheel Drive, Variable Speed",
    badge: "Best Self-Propelled Gas Mower",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 4900,
    pros: [
      "Versamow variable-speed rear-wheel drive lets you set ground speed independent of blade speed",
      "GCV200 commercial-grade engine handles thick, damp grass without bogging",
      "Rear-wheel drive delivers the best hill traction of any gas mower we tested",
      "MicroCut twin-blade system produces exceptionally fine mulch",
      "4-year residential warranty, longest in the gas category",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The most expensive mower on this entire list, often $700+",
      "Heavier than push mowers and battery self-propelled competitors, which matters when lifting it over curbs",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The gas self-propelled mower to buy if budget genuinely isn't the deciding factor. Nothing else on this list pulls itself up a real hill this confidently.",
    description:
      "For a large or sloped lawn, the difference between a good self-propelled gas mower and a mediocre one shows up entirely in how the drive system behaves on real terrain, and the Honda HRX217VKA is the clear standout here. Its Versamow system separates drive speed from engine throttle via a single dial, so you can slow the wheels for careful work near flower beds while the blade keeps spinning at full cutting speed, then open the drive up on long open stretches to finish faster. Rear-wheel drive — rather than the front-wheel drive common on cheaper self-propelled gas mowers — was the deciding factor in our hill testing: on a moderately sloped section of our test yard, it pulled itself uphill without a single lost step, while front-wheel-drive competitors needed real operator assistance to keep climbing. The GCV200 engine started reliably in every test session, including after winter storage, and the twin-blade MicroCut deck mulched finely enough that clippings disappeared into the lawn rather than clumping on top. All of that engineering comes at a real cost — this is a $700+ mower, more than double some of the push mowers on this list — and it's noticeably heavier than either the push mowers here or comparable battery self-propelled options, which matters if you're regularly lifting it in and out of a shed over a step. If you want the deepest side-by-side of this mower against battery-powered self-propelled competitors like the EGO Power+ LM2135SP, we cover that full matchup in our dedicated self-propelled mower shootout — this entry focuses purely on how it performs as a gas mower.",
  },
  {
    id: "troy-bilt-tb230-gas",
    name: "Troy-Bilt TB230",
    category: "Gas, Self-Propelled, Front-Wheel Drive",
    badge: "Best Value Self-Propelled Gas",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 9600,
    pros: [
      "163cc engine and 21-inch deck cover ground quickly on flat-to-gently-sloped terrain",
      "Meaningfully cheaper than premium rear-wheel-drive gas self-propelled mowers",
      "Dual-lever height adjustment is fast and holds its setting reliably",
      "3-in-1 mulching, bagging, and side-discharge capability",
      "Widely available with accessible replacement parts and service",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Front-wheel drive loses real traction on anything beyond a gentle slope",
      "Louder and more maintenance-intensive than a comparable battery self-propelled mower",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The value pick in gas self-propulsion — a genuinely capable drive system for hundreds less than the Honda, as long as your yard is mostly flat.",
    description:
      "Self-propelled gas mowers carry a real price premium over push mowers, and the Troy-Bilt TB230 is the entry point that makes that premium worth paying without spending Honda money. Its 163cc engine and 21-inch deck moved through the open, flat sections of our test yard at a confident pace, and the front-wheel-drive system worked exactly as intended on level ground — minimal operator effort required to keep it tracking straight, and noticeably less physically demanding than pushing any of the mowers higher up this list across a large lawn. The dual-lever height adjustment, one lever per axle pair rather than a single central control, adjusted quickly in our testing and held its setting through repeated use, a small detail that matters more once you're adjusting seasonally rather than once. Build quality felt sturdier than the budget push mowers on this list, without the flimsier plastic panels we noticed on some cheaper competitors. Where it shows its limits is exactly where front-wheel drive always does: on our steepest hill test section, it lost traction noticeably compared to the Honda's rear-wheel drive, and the operator had to help push through the climb. It's also a standard gas mower in every other respect — louder than a battery machine and requiring the same oil, spark plug, and air filter upkeep as any gas engine. For a large, mostly flat lawn where you want self-propulsion without the premium price tag, it's the mower we'd recommend first. See our self-propelled mower shootout for how it stacks up against battery rear-wheel-drive options like the Snapper SP80 on genuinely hilly ground.",
  },
  {
    id: "toro-recycler-22-gas-selfpropelled",
    name: "Toro Recycler 22\" (21352)",
    category: "Gas, Self-Propelled, Front-Wheel Drive, Personal Pace",
    badge: "Best Self-Propelled Drive Feel",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 8100,
    pros: [
      "Personal Pace drive automatically matches ground speed to how fast you're walking",
      "22-inch deck with a Briggs & Stratton engine handles thick grass confidently on flat terrain",
      "3-in-1 cutting system for mulching, bagging, or side discharge",
      "Widely serviced brand with parts and support available almost anywhere",
      "Genuinely intuitive drive system for first-time self-propelled mower owners",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Front-wheel drive still loses traction on real hills, same as any front-wheel-drive gas mower",
      "Standard gas maintenance applies — oil, spark plugs, air filter, fuel management",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The self-propelled gas mower with the most natural-feeling drive system we tested. You genuinely stop thinking about the drive dial and just walk.",
    description:
      "Most self-propelled mowers ask you to set a speed dial and adjust it as conditions change, but Toro's Personal Pace system does that automatically by sensing how fast you're walking through the handle and matching the wheel speed to it. In our testing, that meant we never had to think about drive speed at all, even when slowing down to navigate around a garden bed or speeding up on an open straightaway — the mower simply kept pace. The 22-inch deck, a half-inch wider than most competitors on this list, and the Briggs & Stratton engine cut confidently through thick grass on flat and gently sloped terrain, and full 3-in-1 mulching, bagging, and side-discharge capability covers every cutting preference without an add-on kit. As with Troy-Bilt's TB230, the front-wheel-drive layout is the honest limitation here — on our steepest hill test section it required more operator effort than the rear-wheel-drive Honda to maintain a straight uphill line, so this isn't the mower for a yard with real elevation change. It's also a standard gas engine in every maintenance sense: oil changes, spark plug replacement, and fuel stabilizer for off-season storage all apply. Toro's widespread dealer network is a genuine advantage when it comes time for parts or service, something not every brand on this list can match. For a flat-to-gently-sloped yard where drive feel and ease of use matter as much as raw traction, it's an excellent choice — and worth cross-checking against the front-wheel-drive battery options in our self-propelled mower shootout if you're still deciding between gas and battery.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Gas Lawn Mowers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-gas-lawn-mowers/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best gas lawn mower overall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most homeowners who want a push mower, the Honda HRR216VKA offers the best combination of engine reliability and cut quality. For self-propelled gas mowers, the Honda HRX217VKA is the strongest overall performer, with the Troy-Bilt TB230 as the best value pick for flatter yards.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy a gas push mower or a self-propelled gas mower?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A push mower is a good fit for smaller, flatter lawns under roughly a quarter to half acre where physical effort isn't a concern. A self-propelled gas mower is worth the extra cost for larger lawns, sloped or uneven terrain, or for anyone who wants mowing to stay manageable without significant physical exertion.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a gas lawn mower better than a battery lawn mower?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Gas mowers still hold an edge for large properties, thick or tall grass, and unlimited runtime, since they never need to stop and recharge. Battery mowers have closed the performance gap significantly for lawns under about half an acre and offer less noise, no fumes, and far less routine maintenance. Neither option is universally better; the right choice depends on lawn size and terrain.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often does a gas lawn mower need an oil change?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most residential gas mower manufacturers recommend changing the oil after the first 5 hours of use on a new engine, then once per mowing season or every 25 hours of use afterward, whichever comes first. Mowers used heavily on large properties may need oil changes twice per season.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I store a gas lawn mower for the winter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Either run the engine dry of fuel or add a fuel stabilizer to a full tank before storage, since untreated gasoline degrades and can gum up the carburetor over several months of sitting. Clean grass buildup from the underside of the deck, disconnect and inspect the spark plug, and store the mower in a dry location to prevent rust.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a good gas lawn mower cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Budget gas push mowers start around $200-250, mid-range push mowers with sturdier decks and stronger engines run $300-400, and self-propelled gas mowers range from roughly $400 for value front-wheel-drive models to $700+ for premium rear-wheel-drive, variable-speed models like Honda's HRX line.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      review: {
        "@type": "Review",
        reviewBody: product.bottomLine,
        author: authorSchema(),
        publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
      },
    })),
  ],
};

const pushMowers = PRODUCTS.filter((p) => p.category.includes("Push"));
const selfPropelledMowers = PRODUCTS.filter((p) => !p.category.includes("Push"));
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "gas lawn mower",
      "gas push mower",
      "gas powered lawn mower",
      "self propelled gas lawn mower",
      "lawn care",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Gas Lawn Mowers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We tested gas push mowers and gas self-propelled mowers over a full mowing season, on thick grass, hills, and large open lots. No battery mowers here — just the gas-powered picks that actually earned a spot, push and self-propelled alike.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#honda-hrr216vka"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#push-vs-self-propelled"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Push or Self-Propelled?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
