        <!-- SECTION 1: Gas vs Battery vs Reel -->
        <div id="gas-vs-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Gas vs Battery vs Reel/Electric Mowers — Why Gas Still Wins for Some Yards
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is a gas-only guide, and deliberately so — battery, corded electric, and reel mowers each deserve their own comparison rather than a token mention here. But before recommending any gas powered lawn mower, it's worth being honest about when gas is actually the right call and when it isn't.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gas mowers</strong> win decisively on unlimited runtime, since a fuel tank refills in seconds rather than requiring an hour or more on a charger. They also retain a real edge in raw cutting power through thick, tall, or damp grass, and on hilly or uneven terrain, where a strong self-propelled gas engine has more sustained torque than most battery drive systems can match over a long mowing session. For lawns pushing toward half an acre and beyond, or properties with genuinely thick St. Augustine or Bermuda grass, gas remains the more dependable choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery mowers</strong> have closed the gap dramatically over the last several product generations, and for smaller, flatter lawns under about half an acre, a good 56V or 80V battery mower is now genuinely comparable to a mid-tier gas mower on cutting power, while being quieter, fume-free, and far lower maintenance. If that sounds like a better fit for your yard, our <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">self-propelled mower shootout</a> covers battery picks like the EGO Power+ LM2135SP and Snapper SP80 head-to-head against gas, and our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-small-yard/">small yard mower guide</a> covers battery, corded, and reel options sized for smaller lots.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Reel and corded electric mowers</strong> are worth a brief mention for completeness: reel mowers are the cheapest and lowest-maintenance option for a small, flat, well-maintained lawn, but they struggle with thick or tall grass and aren't a fit for anything beyond roughly a quarter acre. Corded electric mowers offer unlimited runtime like gas without the fumes, but the cord itself becomes a genuine hassle on any yard with obstacles, trees, or more than a small, simple footprint. Neither is a serious alternative to gas for a mid-size to large lawn with any real grass thickness.
          </p>

