          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Push vs. Self-Propelled Gas Mowers — Which Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every gas mower on the market falls into one of two categories, and picking the wrong one is the single most common regret we hear about from readers. A <strong>gas push mower</strong> relies entirely on you to move it forward; the engine only powers the blade. A <strong>self-propelled gas mower</strong> uses a small transmission connected to the engine to drive the wheels, so you're guiding the mower rather than pushing its full weight across the lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a small, flat lot, that difference is barely noticeable, and a lighter push mower is often easier to maneuver around tight obstacles. On anything larger, sloped, or covered in thick grass, self-propulsion turns a genuinely draining chore into something closer to a fifteen-minute task. The decision comes down to three factors: yard size, terrain, and your own physical stamina for the job.
          </p>

