        <div id="engine-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Engine Size and Cutting Width Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas powered lawn mower listings lean heavily on two specs — engine displacement in cubic centimeters (cc) and deck width in inches — and both matter more than most buyers realize when comparing options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Engine Displacement (cc)</h3>
          <p>
            A typical residential gas mower engine ranges from about 140cc on budget push mowers up to 190-200cc on premium self-propelled models. More displacement generally means more torque, which translates directly into how well a mower handles thick, tall, or damp grass without bogging down or stalling. For a small, regularly-mowed lawn, a 140-150cc engine is genuinely sufficient. For a larger lawn, infrequent mowing that lets grass grow taller between cuts, or thicker grass varieties, stepping up to 160cc or higher pays off in fewer stalls and a cleaner cut.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Deck Width</h3>
          <p>
            Most residential gas mowers use a 20 to 22-inch deck, and the difference sounds small but compounds across an entire lawn. A 22-inch deck covers roughly 10% more ground per pass than a 20-inch deck, which adds up to meaningfully fewer passes on a large property. The trade-off is maneuverability — a wider deck is harder to pivot tightly around trees, beds, and other obstacles, so a smaller, more obstacle-heavy yard often does better with a narrower deck despite the extra passes required.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blade-Brake-Clutch (Push Mowers)</h3>
          <p>
            On push mowers, a blade-brake-clutch — sometimes marketed as Roto-Stop — lets the engine keep idling while the blade stops spinning when you release the safety bail, rather than killing the whole engine. It's a small feature that saves a real amount of frustration over a mowing season, since you're not restarting the engine from scratch every time you clear a clog or step away momentarily.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Maintenance -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gas Mower Maintenance Basics
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the section battery mower owners get to skip entirely, and it's worth being upfront about before you buy: a gas lawn mower requires real, ongoing maintenance to keep running reliably for years rather than degrading into an unreliable starter within a couple of seasons.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Most Common Gas Mower Problem</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              If a gas mower won't start after sitting unused for a season, the most likely cause is stale gasoline gumming up the carburetor. Gasoline begins breaking down within about 30 days and can leave a varnish-like residue behind as it evaporates. Draining the tank or using a fuel stabilizer before storage prevents this almost entirely, and it's the single cheapest maintenance habit that saves the most headaches.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil Changes</h3>
          <p>
            Change the oil after the first 5 hours of use on a brand-new engine, then once per season or every 25 hours of use afterward, whichever comes first. Running an engine low on oil or on old, degraded oil is one of the fastest ways to shorten its lifespan, and it's a five-minute job on most residential mowers once you've done it a couple of times.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Spark Plugs</h3>
          <p>
            A worn spark plug is a common cause of hard starting, rough idling, or reduced power, and most manufacturers recommend replacing it annually or every 25 hours of use. Spark plugs are inexpensive and take only a few minutes to swap, and it's worth doing proactively at the start of each mowing season rather than waiting for starting problems to show up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Air Filter</h3>
          <p>
            A clogged air filter starves the engine of airflow, reducing power and fuel efficiency and, in severe cases, causing hard starting or stalling. Paper filters should generally be replaced once per season; foam filters can often be cleaned and reused a few times before replacement. Check it more frequently if you're mowing in dusty conditions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Off-Season Storage and Fuel Stabilizer</h3>
          <p>
            Before storing a gas mower for winter, either run the tank dry by letting the engine run until it stops on its own, or fill the tank and add a fuel stabilizer to prevent the gasoline from degrading over several months of inactivity. Clean packed grass and debris from the underside of the deck, disconnect the spark plug wire as a safety precaution, and store the mower somewhere dry to prevent rust on the deck and blade. Skipping this step is the single most common reason gas mowers won't start reliably each spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this is difficult, but it is real, recurring work that a battery mower simply doesn't require — no oil, no spark plugs, no fuel stabilizer, no carburetor to gum up. If that ongoing upkeep sounds like more than you want to deal with, it's worth reading our <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">self-propelled mower guide</a> for battery alternatives before committing to gas.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes When Buying a Gas Lawn Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas mowers are simple machines, but the buying decision has more nuance than it first appears, and these are the mistakes we see most often.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Push Mower for a Large or Sloped Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A push mower that feels perfectly manageable on a demo floor becomes genuinely exhausting across a large or sloped real-world lawn. If your property is over a quarter acre or has any real elevation change, budget for self-propulsion rather than assuming you'll adjust — most people who make this mistake end up buying a second, self-propelled mower within a year or two anyway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Fuel Stabilizer Before Storage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Leaving old gasoline sitting in the tank and carburetor over winter is the single most common cause of a gas mower that won't start in spring. A bottle of fuel stabilizer costs a few dollars and takes thirty seconds to add before your last mow of the season — skipping it can turn into a $60-150 carburetor cleaning or repair bill.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Drive Type on a Self-Propelled Model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Not all self-propelled gas mowers are equal on hills. Front-wheel-drive models are cheaper and fine for flat ground, but lose real traction on any noticeable slope. If your yard has genuine elevation change, prioritize rear-wheel drive even though it costs more — it's the spec that determines whether self-propulsion actually delivers on hills.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Ongoing Maintenance Costs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gas mower's sticker price is only part of the real cost. Between fuel, annual oil changes, spark plugs, and air filters, expect to spend roughly $60-150 per year keeping a gas mower running well. Factor that into the comparison against battery alternatives, which carry almost none of that ongoing cost, before assuming gas is the cheaper choice long-term.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas lawn mower overall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners who want a push mower, the Honda HRR216VKA offers the best combination of engine reliability and cut quality. For self-propelled gas mowers, the Honda HRX217VKA is the strongest overall performer, with the Troy-Bilt TB230 as the best value pick for flatter yards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a gas push mower or a self-propelled gas mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A push mower is a good fit for smaller, flatter lawns under roughly a quarter to half acre where physical effort isn't a concern. A self-propelled gas mower is worth the extra cost for larger lawns, sloped or uneven terrain, or for anyone who wants mowing to stay manageable without significant physical exertion.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a gas lawn mower better than a battery lawn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas mowers still hold an edge for large properties, thick or tall grass, and unlimited runtime, since they never need to stop and recharge. Battery mowers have closed the performance gap significantly for lawns under about half an acre and offer less noise, no fumes, and far less routine maintenance. Neither option is universally better; the right choice depends on lawn size and terrain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does a gas lawn mower need an oil change?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential gas mower manufacturers recommend changing the oil after the first 5 hours of use on a new engine, then once per mowing season or every 25 hours of use afterward, whichever comes first. Mowers used heavily on large properties may need oil changes twice per season.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I store a gas lawn mower for the winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Either run the engine dry of fuel or add a fuel stabilizer to a full tank before storage, since untreated gasoline degrades and can gum up the carburetor over several months of sitting. Clean grass buildup from the underside of the deck, disconnect and inspect the spark plug, and store the mower in a dry location to prevent rust.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a good gas lawn mower cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Budget gas push mowers start around $200-250, mid-range push mowers with sturdier decks and stronger engines run $300-400, and self-propelled gas mowers range from roughly $400 for value front-wheel-drive models to $700+ for premium rear-wheel-drive, variable-speed models like Honda's HRX line.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

